6" "basis" 1418 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040149/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-13148" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A.$ \n\nLet's start by listing all the elements of $A{:}$\n\n$$\n1,\\qquad 2, \\qquad 3\n$$\n\nNote that only *unique* elements are counted. In other words, duplicate elements are *not* counted separately.\n\nSince $A$ has $3$ elements, its cardinality is $|A| = 3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040149/example-13148.json" "file_sha256" "c868819c82e28e4c7ec2f3b80c564a1f0f34fe5847b785da0e9108adb5b7b9f6" "basis" 1418 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040149/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-13149" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is the cardinal number of $X = \\{ a, \\emptyset, \\{\\emptyset\\}, \\{\\{\\emptyset\\}\\}, b \\}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040149/example-13149.json" "file_sha256" "13cefd531e2a1331c72fecc656681a901667101e67421eeaed8d404fd402362e" "basis" 1418 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040149/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-13149" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The cardinality of a finite set $X$ is the number of elements in $X.$ \n\nLet's start by listing all the elements of $X{:}$\n\n$$\na, \\qquad \\emptyset, \\qquad \\{\\emptyset\\}, \\qquad \\{\\{\\emptyset\\}\\}, \\qquad b\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- Only *unique* elements are counted. In other words, duplicate elements are *not* counted separately.\n\n- $\\emptyset, \\{\\emptyset\\}$ and $\\{\\{\\emptyset\\}\\}$ each count as single, distinct elements of $X.$\n\nSince $X$ has $5$ elements, the cardinality is $|X| = 5.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040149/example-13149.json" "file_sha256" "13cefd531e2a1331c72fecc656681a901667101e67421eeaed8d404fd402362e" "basis" 1418 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040149/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-13169" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $A = \\{10, 19, 28,\\ldots,865\\},$ then what is $|A|?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040149/example-13169.json" "file_sha256" "81d7a69d2a2bddb9bd2f47ddbf1067f8a6567e92f3925aa1e2d7d764907c1390" "basis" 1418 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040149/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-13169" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A.$ \n\nNotice that the elements of our set \n\n$$\n10, \\quad 19, \\quad 28,\\quad \\ldots\n$$\n\nform an arithmetic sequence with a common difference $d=9{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =9 \\\\  & =19-10 \\\\  & =28-19 \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our arithmetic sequence, denoted $a_n,$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =10+(n-1)\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =10+9n-9 \\\\  & =9n+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={9n+1:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le N}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $N\\in \\mathbb N$ is to be determined.\n\nTo find $N,$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9N+1 & =865 \\\\ 9N & =864 \\\\ N & =\\frac{864}{9} \\\\ N & =96\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our set $A$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nA = \\left\\{9n+1: n\\in \\mathbb N,\\: 1\\leq n \\leq 96  \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A$ has $96$ elements, we must have $|A| = 96.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040149/example-13169.json" "file_sha256" "81d7a69d2a2bddb9bd2f47ddbf1067f8a6567e92f3925aa1e2d7d764907c1390" "basis" 1418 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040149/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-13172" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $A = \\{3, 21, 147,1\\,029,\\ldots,5\\,931\\,980\\,229\\},$ then what is $|A|?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040149/example-13172.json" "file_sha256" "166ff9c0f4a49ff3de6d37416acee1d2e6c52523ca69d7d2e5ad80d4844b73fe" "basis" 1418 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040149/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-13172" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A.$ \n\nNotice that the elements of our set \n\n$$\n3, \\quad 21, \\quad 147, \\quad 1\\,029,\\quad \\ldots\n$$\n\nform a geometric sequence with a common ratio $r=7{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =7 \\\\  & =\\frac{21}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{147}{21} \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our geometric sequence, denoted $a_n,$ is given by\n\n$$\na_n = a_1 r^{n-1} = 3(7^{n-1}) = \\dfrac37(7)^n.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as follows:\n\n$$\nA = \\left\\{\\dfrac37(7)^n : n\\in \\mathbb N,\\: 1\\leq n \\leq N \\right\\}\n$$\n\nwhere $N\\in \\mathbb N$ is to be determined.\n\nTo find $N,$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{7}(7)^{N} & =5\\,931\\,980\\,229 \\\\ {7}^{N} & =13\\,841\\,287\\,201 \\\\ N & ={log}_{7}⁡(13\\,841\\,287\\,201) \\\\ N & =\\frac{{log}_{10}⁡(13\\,841\\,287\\,201)}{{log}_{10}⁡(7)} \\\\ N & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our set $A$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nA = \\left\\{\\dfrac37(7)^n : n\\in \\mathbb N,\\: 1\\leq n \\leq 12\\right\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A$ has $12$ elements, we must have $|A| = 12.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040149/example-13172.json" "file_sha256" "166ff9c0f4a49ff3de6d37416acee1d2e6c52523ca69d7d2e5ad80d4844b73fe" "basis" 1418 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040149/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-181551" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $C={5,10,15,…,40},$ then $|C|=${{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071099/state.json" "file_sha256" "33ddcc82728c8b73f06200b96a9cc05ffaa6bf3c13b298466e1bca5d2a7fa3f3"} {"question" "q-181551" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The cardinality of a finite set $C$ is the number of elements in $C.$ \n\nLet's start by listing all the elements of $C:$\n\n$$\n5,\\,10,\\,15,\\,20,\\,25,\\,30,\\,35,\\,40\n$$\n\nSince $C$ has $8$ elements, its cardinality is $|C|=\\text{8}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071099/state.json" "file_sha256" "33ddcc82728c8b73f06200b96a9cc05ffaa6bf3c13b298466e1bca5d2a7fa3f3"} {"question" "q-181551" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071099/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3144a4334782a62050a0811e21313a4a537bc521b0680b93202a42b6753a09db"} {"questi