}\n$$\n\nNote that ${-2,-3,-4}$ and ${9,10,11,12}$ each count as single elements of $T.$\n\nSince $T$ has $3$ elements, the cardinality is $|T|=3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071099/state.json" "file_sha256" "33ddcc82728c8b73f06200b96a9cc05ffaa6bf3c13b298466e1bca5d2a7fa3f3"} {"question" "q-181557" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071099/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3144a4334782a62050a0811e21313a4a537bc521b0680b93202a42b6753a09db"} {"question" "q-181557" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071099/state.json" "file_sha256" "33ddcc82728c8b73f06200b96a9cc05ffaa6bf3c13b298466e1bca5d2a7fa3f3"} {"question" "q-181557" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071099/state.json" "file_sha256" "33ddcc82728c8b73f06200b96a9cc05ffaa6bf3c13b298466e1bca5d2a7fa3f3"} {"question" "q-80735" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $A={4,{6,11},5,{7,13,15},{8,2}}$, then $|A|=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071099/state.json" "file_sha256" "33ddcc82728c8b73f06200b96a9cc05ffaa6bf3c13b298466e1bca5d2a7fa3f3"} {"question" "q-80735" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A.$ \n\nLet's start by listing all the elements of $A:$\n\n$$\n4,\\,{6,11},\\,5,\\,{7,13,15},\\,{8,2}\n$$\n\nNote that ${6,11}$,  ${7,13,15}$ and ${8,2}$ each count as single elements of $A.$